#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77416e0f-a4f7-5387-809d-57d5f319e686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1)/(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273536} {:answer/id #uuid "f45120cd-50e8-5fbc-b959-25063b17a4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273537} {:answer/id #uuid "d0359b7c-3a01-544c-8df6-4b78eb2ea081" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− (1)/(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273538} {:answer/id #uuid "c22729bd-c52f-52fa-8ebb-5808a6d1ff2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273539} {:answer/id #uuid "522f04f4-f787-54e6-8424-1b540557ab5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f45120cd-50e8-5fbc-b959-25063b17a4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273537} :answer-field/id #uuid "0133a190-bb4f-5b6e-98e2-c14e36c918f4" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fbff772-e6e5-5110-9b66-ccc497ff0d44" :question/math-academy-id "q-75829" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{'}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{'}(3).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{'}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-9$ | $0$ |\n| $5$ | $3$ | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{'}(3)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=5$ and ${f}^{'}(5)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{'}(3) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}(5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
